I recently played the latest  installments of life is strange tales  from the borderlands and dreamfall  chapters now these are all episodic  games but they've been some of my most  enjoyable and anticipated experiences  this year but is the episodic model  actually any good now we shouldn't have  any false illusions about this episodic  games are really only around because it  works extraordinarily well for  publishers with the high cost of game  production the episodic model is a good  way for publishers to make money to help  fund continual and future development  it's a faster more condensed cycle the  sales from one episode enabled them to  keep making the content they have  planned more comfortably the publisher  also gets the advantages of player  feedback time to implement that feedback  more money to support additional  development time and more prolonged  consumer interest than they ordinarily  would get if they did it in one big go  they eventually get a full game package  out and everybody goes home happy right  well yes and no but the audience it  comes down to what you personally value  in games but first let's take a look at  how the episodic model has changed to  become what it is today the beginnings  of episodic game content had clear  aspirations to mimic the way TV shows  are distributed what's interesting is  the kind of media they wanted to mimic  changing over time and how that affected  the overall format telltale Games's  early titles paint a pretty clear  picture of how the episodic gaming model  used to be handled Ricardo Sanchez who  was the VP of one of Telltale's  distributors at the time gametap once  said he had three rules for episodic  content the episodes must be short  standalone and delivered regularly and  within a defined period of time series  such as waltz and grommets grand  adventures strong Bad's cool game for  attractive people and three seasons of  salmon max were all made with this  philosophy telltale wore their TV  influences on their sleeve as well by  making four seasons of CSI adventure  James and even one based on Lauren order  before they started moving into  serialized film based stories with back  to the future and Jurassic Park you can  probably see where I'm going with this  that standalone episodes philosophies  was replaced by games could feature one  major mainline narrative serialized into  several episodes czo present-day  telltale adventures like tales from the  borderlands and the wolf among us as  well as games like life is strange  Resident Evil Revelations 2 and any  other episodic game you can think of  that keeps the story rolling forward  even though they may sometimes opt to  alter character perspectives or forego  linear time once in a while the fact the  telltale are now making games based off  the Walking Dead and Game of Thrones is  a pretty good indication of the  different style of television approach  they're taking now it's about wanting to  find out what comes next in the big  season long story so one of the general  pros and cons although publishers having  more time and money to create the  individual segments as they go is a huge  plus the benefits to using this model  don't stop there  publishers are also able to take  advantage of having several release  dates for each individual episode to  yell about on the internet that extends  the marketing period and prolongs  consumer interests or at least awareness  and should a whole season of episodes be  received positively it lends itself to  the creation of subsequent seasons  Walking Dead Season 3 is a sure thing  for instance so yay for the publishers  but what about us would I rather have  the entirety of dreamful chapters to  play through at my leisure sure but all  those benefits for the publisher really  do end up ultimately benefiting the  gamer as well let's use dreamfall  chapters as an example red thread games  had a successful Kickstarter which  launched with the intent of creating  dreamfall chapters as a full game the  time to produce the game on top of  additional development demands of  meeting Kickstarter stretch goals would  have meant that waiting for the whole  game was going to take years because of  that it ultimately worked out better for  red thread to release the game in  segments since you know we actually get  to play it now  creative director regnal tourist  explained as a time quote we've also  realized that while as a small studio we  are capable of producing a massively  ambitious game like dreamfall chapters  we're not satisfied with our ability to  properly finish polish and bug fix the  whole game in one go by releasing book 1  we hope to start generating income all  of which will go right back into  improving the quality and scope of  subsequent books so in addition to  getting a bit of the game sooner we'll  hopefully receive a better game than we  would have if it it all come out in one  go episodic games can be attractive to  consumers for more intangible benefits -  like if you don't have much time to  commit to gaming and want a story based  game you can enjoy for a couple of hours  or if you like the idea of being able to  buy the first episode of a game and see  if you actually like it before  committing to buying the whole season of  course those advantages also bring  disadvantages the big one is ambiguous  release dates rarely do episodic games  have predetermined - release dates  anymore since they're increasing in  scope and the next episode is usually  being developed as you're playing the  current one like in the case of  dreamfall chapters Resident Evil  Revelations 2 worked quite differently  as Capcom essentially had it all  finished before its first episode was  out but this is certainly the exception  and not the rule  sometimes subsequent episodes have  pushed back and sometimes like in the  case of Sonic the Hedgehog for the first  two episodes just didn't get the  reception the publisher wanted and no  more were produced personally I'm a big  fan of the episodic model because the  caters towards the kind of games that I  love to play that it won't work for  everything and that's okay that said it  is a good way for smaller devs to be  able to slowly but surely build out big  ideas and pull in an audience along the  way  but that's just my opinion so be sure to  head down to that comment section and  let me know if you think it's a good  development and distribution model and  why or why not
 

